7/7/95  Conversation with Ratıp Esen -

Had been Muhtar from 1952-56 and 1968-73. Also worked at Ihtiyar Heyeti...
Knows a lot about the history of the village...He was a master carpenter when he came to YH.  He facilitated the building of the new mosque, in 1968. 
What changes happened since 1930’s in agriculture and herding? I came here in 1936, it was spring, from Kıbrıscık. I came to pay tax/head of sheep which we were sending with a shepherd from K. every year to graze here during winter (Nov.-May).( In summer, the flocks went to Köroglu, which is  YAYLA for Kıbrıscık). Then, agriculture was not big here. 
There were about 15 HH here.
In 1940 I came for a wedding of Erdogan Tokat’s uncle.  In 1942 I got engaged to Müyesser. In 1942, State Agricultural Office rented out tractors and other equipment to the farmers at YH. All wealthy resident herders tried out tractors and did wheat..The result in 1942 was so spectacular that Mehmet Ali Aga rented out a whole bar for a day, and paid 4,500 TL. Lots of money back then. (Wheat cost 18-19 kurus/kg.). So, mechanized agriculture started in 1942, on a small scale; there were about 20-25 HH, then.  A little bit of herding, combined with some farming was going on. 
So, we set up relationships with the residents here. In 1949 tractors were sold to people...One was Fazlı Kayran. In 1950’s, benefiting from Marshall Plan, 
under Menderes, many people were able to buy tractors, used it on the farm.
Farms were not owned in general. Only some fields near Sakarya were owned with deeds, in 1937. Mostly, people farmed wherever they wanted to. Grazing was getting less.. In 1950, due to excavations a lot of people came here as workers, and they settled here. Further, they farmed....
The “ Mevki” names were given according to landowners’ names..

Land Use: The earliest farmland was near Sakarya.. The related families lived near each other so as to form cooperative labor units. (But now most of them don’t cooperate, with the exception of Köseoglu, Coban and a few others.)
Land is farmed, generally, by people who own land and rent it from Hazine. Sharecropping is another strategy used by farmers who do not own tools or machinery. In addition, families who lack labor force will either rent their land to others or engage in sharecropping.

Land Acquisition: Prior to 1953 the families farmed as much land as they wanted and wherever they wanted which was mostly near Sakarya. In 1953 the distributed land was random-- people had no choice to select, according to soil quality. They took what they could get. Each HH got 190 dönüm, some fields at one place, others at 2, 3 or more locations. Nobody objected.. It was mostly dry farming. Another way of acquiring land was to farm it for at least 30 years, without a deed, until the State let you have it  (“zilliyet hakkı”). Now, each HH will be eligible to get 40-50 dönüm, with the new distribution. 
In 1953 distribution of land was random-- people had no choice to select, according to soil quality. They took what they could get. Each HH got 190 dönüms, some fields at one place, others at 2, 3 or more locations. Nobody objected.. It was mostly dry farming. Another way of acquiring land was to farm it for at least 30 years, without a deed, until the State let you have it  (“zilliyet hakkı”). Now, each HH will be eligible to get 40-50 dönüm, with the new distribution. 
How many sheep did you have? I had 150 sheep, c. 350 dönüm land..Now I sold all the sheep.  
Once the population grew, people rented farm from Treasury, farming intensified with the help of technology and fertilizer etc.. How did the people keep up with more and more productivity? They began to find alternative jobs, got education, so did not depend on farming...For ex. We have 13 children, 450 dönüm land..each would get c. 34.5 dönüm..that’s nothing...  
Both farmer’s and herder’s lives are very risky:
{“Davarın altından yel gecer, bir kısda biter. “}
{Bir dolu olur, bütün tarlayı kasıp kavurur}

So, choose crops with least risk..e.g. sugar beet cannot be affected by hail.
But wheat and barley would be totally destroyed.

When there is no hope for expansion, what do you do? You plant the crop that is least risky and most productive..barley is very productive on this soil...
That’s what I count on. The best strategy is mixed agriculture. Takviyen olacak, calısacak adamın olacak, kendin de cok calısacaksın. But there is a limit, too.
Coban, Karagöz ve Salim Kayran aileleri are very unsociable, they only work and live for money. 

It is interesting that distribution of families is according to place of origin:
from Midas tumulus west toward Ratip Esen’s house, all except 3 HH are from Beypazarı; and people from Kıbrıscık are west of the new mosque.
All living in lumps.....
Two Kurdish families,  Fikret Beklemez..His father, Ekrem, is Kurdish on father’s side, not Kurdish on mother’s side...Muharrem (Kezban’s husband)
is Ekrem’s wife’s  brother..So, that’s two..Kürt Fatma (Caglar).

Why are some gardens bigger than others, also, courtyards vary in size?
Initially, they    were all 1.5 dönüms.. The front gardens and courtyards were the same....But, they expanded from the back..illegally.  Some are even 3 dönüms now. It was not controlled after ‘70s. For example, Ekrem’s stepmother has rounded up 7 dönüms of land...


HERDING : 

Herding Strategies: 
1) select for no. of lambs rather than secondary products
2) seasonal transhumance (SÖ, ÇK, Y. Köseler, Ömerler)--more grazing than feeding
3) keep flocks on “yayla” during agricult. growth season
4) diversify: breed milk cows, fowl, rabbits etc..
5) collective herding--add others’ flocks to one’s own & collect wages, kerme


Balance between Herding and Agriculture: 
	a) primarily herding, fringe agriculture- land rented to grow barley & 		fodder; c. 50 d. land, average flock size: 150-200
	b) primarily agricult., herding for surplus (cash)- c.250-800 d.land;
		average flock size 80, 3-10 milk cows.





There is still have a very big YAYLA at Köroglu Mtns, near Bolu....There are 80-100 herds seen grazing in the summer... Kıbrıscık is c. 150 km from here..one can walk from there with herds in one week...staying overnight at known agıls. But the return trip takes longer because they walk with young lambs, slowly.
The key reason why herding is diminishing is the absence of shepherds...
In Kıbrıscık shepherds were trained as young kids “cekirdekten yetisme”..Now, noone wants to be a shepherd.

How did you end up with Merinos, through artificial insemination? About 30 years ago, they gave us imported Merinos rams..we injected Karaman sheep with Merinos semen...the first 3,4 generations were strange, they had little hair, bigger than Karaman, but ate a lot and was very strong.  After 5 generations Merinos was achieved.
Growing fodder and other feed for the animals is not done-- it was tried before, but not continued--- yonca, soya beans, corn--
Presently, there is 4,000 d. of grazing land in 3 areas: Kızlarkayası (to the north), Aksuvat(              ) and Sülüklü (southeast).


